A||N||3||kornholio||Occasional starter grind||||1||
Z||000000||kornholio||05-17-2001||09:43 PM||||Some times (very rare) when I turn my key I get this terrible grinding noise from the starter.  When this happens I release the key, and try to start again and it starts right up.  Anyone know what's wrong?  This happens once every couple months.  I thought I really stripped all the gears on my starter today...I used my remote start and it just kept grinding it for like 5 seconds.  Scared the crap out of me.<BR>Thanks in advance.||||reg||1
Z||000001||PBJ||05-18-2001||12:45 AM||||sounds like the ring gear on your flywheel is worn out.  If you have a six cylinder the flywheel wears heavily in six spots-at compression stroke.  So when it grinds and than you release and re try you have turned the mtr over enough to catch good teeth.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||05-18-2001||08:50 AM||||Its also possible the bendix gear isn't doing its job and isn't springing out to bite the flywheel.<P>I would remove all the plugs, take off the starter, and have someone slowly rotate the crank while you watch the flywheel. If the flywheel looks good, you might be able to spary a little lube on the starter shaft(I don't know if that is good for the starter, ask someone) to allow the gear to spring a litle more freely. The starter could just be gunked up real bad, might just need to be cleaned so the gear can spring more freely.||||reg||1
Z||000003||NASAReeves||05-18-2001||06:59 PM||||I had the same problem a while back.  My problem was the starter (bendix).  I replaced the brushes in the starter, and WD-40ed the Bendix.  It didn't do the trick...so I had to replace the whole starter.  Since the replacement...no problem.  <P>Note:  I inspected the flywheel too, as recommended earlier, while the starter was off.  It looked fine.||||reg||1
